const body = `<h1>9.1b — Writing Subclasses: Extend and Customize</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Character Hierarchy</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you explored IS-A relationships. Today you'll build the hierarchy: a base Character class and subclasses that inherit from it.</p>
        <p>Create <code>Character</code> (parent):</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Protected fields: <code>String name</code>, <code>int hp</code>, <code>int attack</code>.</li>
            <li>Constructor takes name, hp, attack.</li>
            <li>Methods: <code>void takeDamage(int dmg)</code>, <code>void attack(Character target)</code>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Create <code>Warrior extends Character</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Additional field: <code>int armor</code>.</li>
            <li>Constructor: calls <code>super(name, hp, attack)</code>, then sets armor.</li>
            <li>Override <code>takeDamage()</code>: reduce damage by armor first (minimum 0 damage).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Create <code>Mage extends Character</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Additional field: <code>int mana</code>.</li>
            <li>Override <code>attack()</code>: if mana >= 10, deal double damage and spend 10 mana. Otherwise normal attack.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/extends-and-super.html">Inheritance</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/class-design.html">Writing Classes</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
